# The three-star question: two characters' changes side by side, each proved with a line from near
# the start and one from near the end, and what changed them. A grown-up marks it.
item reading.garden-change v=1 stars=3 skills=[reading.characters, reading.evidence, reading.whole-book, writing.composing] {
  title "Two children change"

  scene 35x35 {
    column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
      row top space=1 align=end {
        bookcast m who=mary pose=walk dir=1
        robin r post=1 facing=-1
        flowers f count=3 petals=5
      }
      text ask "Mary and Colin both change. For each of them, copy a few words from near the start of the book and a few from near the end that show the change, and say what changed them." width=34
      writinglines lines lines=5 width=34 prompt=""
    }
  }

  check writing.by-eye look-for="Both changes named, each with two short quotations copied exactly: Mary from sour, cross and unloved (a sour expression, quite contrary, the most disagreeable-looking child) to kind and eager; Colin from sure he will die (no one believes I shall live to grow up, screaming, a lump) to strong (as straight as an arrow, I shall live forever, burst through it at full speed). What changed them is named: the garden, the moor air and work, Dickon and his mother, each other, and for Colin the Magic he believes in." notice=["Says how Mary changes", "Says how Colin changes", "A quotation from the start and the end for each", "Says what changed them"] ask="Which of the two changes more, do you think?"
  hint "How is Mary described on the very first page? How is she by the last chapters?"
  hint "What did Colin believe about himself when Mary met him? What does he do in the last chapter?"
}
